  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface over x, z from -2 to 2. It is 3 times steeper in x, so overshooting appears there first (beyond learning rate 0.67 the x swings grow).",
    "Simulated noise: Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with sd 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b. It is exactly zero at 64. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same sequence.",
    "The orange arrow is the next actual step: −learning rate × noisy slope. The grey arrow is −learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn at 2× their true length, and arrows are hidden once a run ends.",
    "A run ends as finished after 40 updates, or as diverged when the loss goes above 8. The ball is then drawn at the bowl's edge, but the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The host's clock makes one update every 6 ticks, which is 5 updates per second at 1× speed. If you change a setting mid-run, the remaining updates use the new value."
  ]
}